The verdict verbs' pre-run discipline: exit 2 is "the question could not be
asked", and a failure **before** the question — a session that will not open, a
context that resolves to nothing, a ledger with no registry to walk — must land
there, never on 1. Their 1 is a *verdict*: `cutover`'s 1 says "the old family
still speaks", and reaching it through main's anyhow edge on a dead router told
CI exactly that about a bus nobody listened to.

The fixture is the same dead client-mode bus as `session-transport-fallback`:
`connect.timeout_ms: 0`, `exit_on_failure: true`, so the failure is immediate
and without retry.

```console
$ zenctl cutover --old-root 'old/**' --zenoh-config tests/cmd/fixtures/dead-bus.json5
? 2
Error: failed to open Zenoh session

Caused by:
    Unable to connect to any of [Single(tcp/127.0.0.1:1)]!
cutover: the question could not be asked — exit 2, the reserved non-verdict (1 here would claim a verdict this run never reached)

```

`expect` reserves the same 2 ("session failure" is in its help text), and now
its session open honours it too — not just the observation window.

```console
$ zenctl expect 'v1/**' --within 1 --zenoh-config tests/cmd/fixtures/dead-bus.json5
? 2
Error: failed to open Zenoh session

Caused by:
    Unable to connect to any of [Single(tcp/127.0.0.1:1)]!
expect: the question could not be asked — exit 2, the reserved non-verdict (1 here would claim a verdict this run never reached)

```

A context that names nothing is a **1** on a listing (`session-config-error`
pins that) — a listing's 1 carries no verdict. On a verdict verb the same
mistake must be a 2, because `why`'s 1 is the claim "no cause found and
everything checked looks healthy".

```console
$ zenctl why 'v1/h-3fa9c2d41b7e/state/sysinfo/health' --context no-such-context
? 2
Error: context "no-such-context" not found in [..]config.toml — `zenctl context list`
why: the question could not be asked — exit 2, the reserved non-verdict (1 here would claim a verdict this run never reached)

```

`registry retired` walks a ledger; no `--registry` means there is no ledger to
walk — unproven, not "still speaks" and not a pass.

```console
$ zenctl registry retired
? 2
Error: registry retired walks the [[deprecated]] ledger of local registry files — pass --registry <dir> (or set one on the active context)
registry retired: the question could not be asked — exit 2, the reserved non-verdict (1 here would claim a verdict this run never reached)

```

`schema check --producer` already had the vocabulary (`not checked:`) and now
routes its session failure through it — a dead bus is silence about the type,
not a claim about the payload.

```console
$ zenctl schema check --type Health --from '{}' --producer sysinfo --zenoh-config tests/cmd/fixtures/dead-bus.json5
? 2
not checked: no session, so sysinfo's served describe is out of reach: failed to open Zenoh session: Unable to connect to any of [Single(tcp/127.0.0.1:1)]!

```

So is the invocation that names no schema source at all — a check that never
happened, which used to come back through `?` as exit 1, this verb's "does not
conform".

```console
$ zenctl schema check --type Health --from '{}'
? 2
not checked: give --producer (live describe) or --schema-set FILE — the registry TOMLs carry type names, not shapes (RFC 08 §7)

```
